To find the de Broglie wavelength we use  

\[
\lambda=\frac{h}{p}, \qquad p=\sqrt{2mK}
\]

where \(K\) is the kinetic energy.

**1. Convert the energy to cgs units**

\[
4.8\ \text{MeV}=4.8\times1.6\times10^{-6}\ \text{erg}=7.68\times10^{-6}\ \text{erg}
\]

**2. Compute the momentum**

\[
p=\sqrt{2mK}= \sqrt{2(6.6\times10^{-24}\ \text{g})(7.68\times10^{-6}\ \text{erg})}
\]

\[
p=\sqrt{1.0138\times10^{-28}}\ \text{g\,cm/s}=1.01\times10^{-14}\ \text{g\,cm/s}
\]

**3. Calculate the wavelength**

\[
h=6.6\times10^{-27}\ \text{erg\,s}=6.6\times10^{-27}\ \text{g\,cm}^2/\text{s}
\]

\[
\lambda=\frac{h}{p}=\frac{6.6\times10^{-27}\ \text{g\,cm}^2/\text{s}}
{1.01\times10^{-14}\ \text{g\,cm/s}}
=6.6\times10^{-13}\ \text{cm}
\]

Thus the de Broglie wavelength of the alpha particle is  

\[
\boxed{6.6\times10^{-13}\ \text{cm}}
\]

Answer: **F**

To find the average atomic mass, weight each isotopic mass by its fractional abundance:

\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{Average} &= 50(0.0431) + 52(0.8376) + 53(0.0955) + 54(0.0238) \\
&= 2.155 + 43.6032 + 5.0615 + 1.2852 \\
&\approx 52.10\ \text{amu}
\end{aligned}
\]

The closest listed value is **52.057 amu**.

